# Prod-environment Caddyfile (docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §3 Session 2). Lives at
# deploy-prod/caddy/Caddyfile, not deploy-prod/Caddyfile (moved here in Session 10) — this
# directory is mounted WHOLE into the caddy container (`./caddy:/etc/caddy:ro` in
# docker-compose.yml), deliberately NOT a single-file bind mount of just this file. A single-file
# mount binds to the file's INODE, not its path; rsync's default replace-via-rename (writing a
# new file then renaming over the old one, same as every deploy's sync step does) swaps the
# inode at that path, and a running container with a single-file mount keeps serving the OLD
# inode's content forever — `caddy reload` reads that same stale bind mount and reports success
# while actually serving nothing new. Confirmed real on dev (Session 10): the host file had the
# new content, the container's own `/etc/caddy/Caddyfile` did not, and only recreating the
# container fixed it. A directory mount doesn't have this problem — the container always resolves
# the path against whatever the host directory currently points to. See docs/deploy-prod.md §12.
#
# Copied from deploy/caddy/Caddyfile (dev) as the stated starting point — differences from that
# file:
#   - No `X-Robots-Tag: noindex` anywhere (every host here is a real, indexable prod host; dev's
#     own file flags this exact line as the one to drop when prod gets its own Caddyfile).
#   - `www.ammari.id` gets its OWN site block that only 301-redirects to the apex — dev instead
#     serves `ammari.my.id`/`www.ammari.my.id` identically from one block. Prod's apex is
#     canonical (docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §2 "www handling" row) so `BETTER_AUTH_URL`/
#     `metadataBase` resolve consistently.
#   - `client_auth`'s `trust_pool` reads prod's OWN zone-level AOP CA
#     (/etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem — a CA we generated ourselves, see
#     cloudflare/README.md), not Cloudflare's published Global AOP CA that dev's identically-named
#     path holds. Different bytes, same filename/mount point, by design (deploy-prod/ is a fully
#     separate directory from deploy/ — docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §2 "Config location" row).
#   - `reverse_proxy` upstreams are NOT a literal container name here — they're an `import` of a
#     generated snippet (deploy-prod/caddy/active-upstream.caddy) that names whichever blue/green
#     slot is currently active. Session 3's deploy script rewrites that file and runs
#     `caddy reload` on every slot flip; this Caddyfile itself never changes for a routine deploy.
#
# The Cloudflare trust boundary lives entirely here, not in ufw (docker publishes its own
# iptables rules that bypass ufw's filtering of published ports — see docs/plans/deploy-dev.md):
#   1. PRIMARY: Cloudflare Authenticated Origin Pulls — `client_auth` below requires a client
#      certificate, signed by OUR OWN CA, on every TLS handshake. A request that didn't actually
#      come through Cloudflare (direct to the VPS IP) is rejected before any application code —
#      or even an HTTP response — runs. This is ZONE-LEVEL AOP (cloudflare/README.md), not the
#      Global toggle dev uses: Cloudflare presents a leaf cert we generated and uploaded
#      ourselves, and only our own CA root (never the leaf's private key) lives on this VPS.
#   2. DEFENSE IN DEPTH: `cloudflare_only` (imported from cloudflare/cloudflare-only.caddy,
#      generated by deploy/update-cloudflare-ips.sh from the shared cloudflare-ips.txt) rejects
#      anything whose remote IP isn't in Cloudflare's published ranges.
#   3. `trusted_proxies` (same source) makes access logs — and `cf-connecting-ip`-based rate
#      limiting in packages/auth / apps/web/src/lib/claim/ip.ts — trust the real client IP only
#      when it actually came from Cloudflare's edge.
#
# VERIFY BEFORE RELYING ON THIS: `caddy validate --config /opt/ammari-prod/Caddyfile` against the
# pinned caddy:2.10.2 image, then the runbook's own "curl the VPS IP directly, bypassing
# Cloudflare, must fail" step (docs/deploy-prod.md, once written in Session 7).
{
	import cloudflare/trusted-proxies.caddy
}

import cloudflare/cloudflare-only.caddy

# Security headers for every PUBLIC site block below (never the bare :8080/:8081 internal-only
# health listeners — HSTS etc. are meaningless over plain internal HTTP and would just be noise
# there). No Content-Security-Policy yet — deliberately deferred, see docs/deploy-prod.md.
# `-Server` drops Caddy's own default `Server: Caddy` response header (confirmed present by
# default via a local `caddy run` + curl — see docs/deploy-prod.md for how this was verified).
#
# Each of these is imported TWICE in its site block below: once for the normal response path,
# and again inside a `handle_errors` block. This is not redundant — confirmed empirically (local
# `caddy run` against a deliberately-unreachable upstream): a `header` directive alone does NOT
# reliably survive a reverse_proxy failure (502) — Caddy's built-in error-response path re-adds
# `Server: Caddy` and drops HSTS/nosniff/Referrer-Policy on that path specifically, even though
# the SAME `header` directive works fine on every normal/404 response. Without the second import
# inside `handle_errors`, an upstream outage would silently serve the one response an attacker is
# most likely to see without these protections.
(web_security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		# SAMEORIGIN, not DENY: nothing in this codebase frames web in an iframe today (admin's
		# own draft-preview flow opens /preview/home in a new tab, not an iframe — see
		# apps/admin/src/app/(shell)/content/_components/homepage-overview.tsx), but SAMEORIGIN
		# still allows web to frame ITS OWN pages later without another Caddyfile change, which
		# DENY would block.
		X-Frame-Options "SAMEORIGIN"
		# geolocation stays fully off — no map-pin/checkout geolocation feature exists in code or
		# docs/plans/*.md yet; flip to `geolocation=(self)` here (and only here) if that ships.
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()"
		-Server
	}
}

(admin_security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		# DENY — admin is never framed, by itself or anyone else; no iframe exists anywhere in
		# this codebase (confirmed by search), so there's no same-origin framing use case to
		# preserve here the way web's own pages might frame each other later.
		X-Frame-Options "DENY"
		Permissions-Policy "camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()"
		-Server
	}
}

# www.ammari.id only ever redirects — no reverse_proxy, so no frame/permissions policy need —
# but keep the same common headers + -Server for consistency.
(www_security_headers) {
	header {
		Strict-Transport-Security "max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains"
		X-Content-Type-Options "nosniff"
		Referrer-Policy "strict-origin-when-cross-origin"
		-Server
	}
}

# Session 3's generated snippet — defines (active_upstream_web) and (active_upstream_admin),
# each a `reverse_proxy` to whichever slot is currently active. The file checked into this repo
# is a sensible DEFAULT pointing at the blue slots (see deploy-prod/caddy/active-upstream.caddy's
# own header comment) so this Caddyfile validates and runs correctly even before Session 3's
# deploy script exists; Session 3 is what actually rewrites it on every slot flip. Lives in this
# SAME directory (not a separate "generated/" mount point) as of Session 10 — see this
# Caddyfile's own new location (deploy-prod/caddy/Caddyfile, not deploy-prod/Caddyfile) and
# docker-compose.yml's single `./caddy:/etc/caddy:ro` directory mount for why.
import active-upstream.caddy

# Internal-only health-check listeners (Session 3, deploy-prod/remote-deploy.sh). Plain HTTP,
# NEVER published to the host (deploy-prod/docker-compose.yml's `caddy` service only publishes
# 80/443) — reachable only via `docker compose exec caddy curl ...` from inside this same
# container. Every PUBLIC site block below requires the Cloudflare Authenticated Origin Pulls
# client certificate (`client_auth` above), which this VPS deliberately never holds (only the CA
# root does — cloudflare/README.md) — so remote-deploy.sh has no way to verify its own
# just-flipped reverse_proxy upstream by hitting ammari.id/admin.ammari.id directly, even from
# localhost on this box. These two bare-port blocks import the SAME generated upstream snippets
# as the real public hosts below, giving the deploy script a genuine end-to-end "did Caddy's
# reload actually pick up the new upstream" check that doesn't require Cloudflare's client cert.
# A bare `:port` address with no hostname never triggers Caddy's automatic HTTPS management, so
# these stay plain HTTP by construction — no cert, no client_auth, nothing to misconfigure here.
:8080 {
	import active_upstream_web
}

:8081 {
	import active_upstream_admin
}

ammari.id {
	import cloudflare_only
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key {
		client_auth {
			mode require_and_verify
			trust_pool file {
				pem_file /etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem
			}
		}
	}
	import web_security_headers
	handle_errors {
		import web_security_headers
		respond "{http.error.status_code}"
	}
	# /api/revalidate is called ONLY over the INTERNAL Docker network (apps/admin ->
	# http://web:3000, bypassing Caddy entirely — see deploy-prod/docker-compose.yml) and never
	# needs the public domain. Blocked here too, defense in depth: Authenticated Origin Pulls
	# above only proves a request came THROUGH Cloudflare, not that it's a legitimate admin
	# request — every ordinary public visitor is proxied through Cloudflare the same way, so
	# without this block a leaked REVALIDATE_SECRET would still work from the public internet.
	# See docs/plans/storefront-cms.md session 6.
	@revalidate path /api/revalidate*
	respond @revalidate 404
	# /api/test/* (apps/web's e2e preview-token backdoor) must NEVER be reachable from the public
	# internet, full stop — it is gated in code (NODE_ENV != production AND
	# E2E_TEST_LOGIN=true, see src/lib/preview/e2e-gate.ts), but that gate lives in an env var
	# that this Caddy config has no visibility into, so this block is NOT conditional on whether
	# the gate currently happens to be closed. It is the one layer that holds even if
	# E2E_TEST_LOGIN were ever mistakenly set in the real web.env on this server.
	@apitest path /api/test/*
	respond @apitest 404
	import active_upstream_web
}

# Apex is canonical (docs/plans/prod-deploy.md §2 "www handling" row) — this host only redirects,
# it never reverse-proxies. Still behind the SAME Cloudflare trust boundary as every other host
# in this file: a direct, Cloudflare-bypassing request to this hostname must fail exactly like
# ammari.id's and admin.ammari.id's do, not quietly serve a redirect to anyone who can reach the
# VPS IP directly. The TLS setup is identical because it's the same zone/cert/CA, not a special
# case — no separate certificate or AOP configuration is needed for this one extra host.
www.ammari.id {
	import cloudflare_only
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key {
		client_auth {
			mode require_and_verify
			trust_pool file {
				pem_file /etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem
			}
		}
	}
	import www_security_headers
	handle_errors {
		import www_security_headers
		respond "{http.error.status_code}"
	}
	redir https://ammari.id{uri} 301
}

admin.ammari.id {
	import cloudflare_only
	tls /etc/caddy/certs/origin.pem /etc/caddy/certs/origin.key {
		client_auth {
			mode require_and_verify
			trust_pool file {
				pem_file /etc/caddy/cloudflare/origin-pull-ca.pem
			}
		}
	}
	import admin_security_headers
	handle_errors {
		import admin_security_headers
		respond "{http.error.status_code}"
	}
	# Same reasoning as web's own /api/test/* block above — apps/admin's e2e sign-in backdoor
	# (/api/test/login) must never be reachable from the public internet regardless of what its
	# own code-level gate currently evaluates to.
	@apitest path /api/test/*
	respond @apitest 404
	import active_upstream_admin
}
